\chapter{Portability and release evidence}
\label{ch:117}
\index{Portability and release evidence}

Release results are specific to compiler revision, source dependencies, configuration, toolchain, operating system and target. Some On1x features run on ordinary native C11, while the cooperative runtime, process captures, certain native modules, and the isolated plugin worker rely on documented host profiles. Illustrative API availability and measured portability are different data.

\section{Construct a release matrix}
Start with the generated and fallback scanners, direct C11 execution, and the supported MIR scalar subset. Add opt-in integrations only where their dependencies can be pinned and built. Label compiler checks, execution tests, sanitizers, and relocation tests distinctly. Include failure behavior for unsupported operations on each target. A recorded skip should say what capability was absent. When recording an unrelated inventory failure, identify its test and verify whether the same failure occurs on the base checkout. Avoid reusing past totals for a release assembled from different sources.

